# Frontend Core

## Context

This project is an MVP for studying and implementing a documentation chatbot.

Frontend stack:

- React
- TypeScript
- Material UI

Backend:

- FastAPI

RAG:

- LlamaIndex

The frontend communicates only with the FastAPI backend.

## MVP Principle

Prioritize:

1. Simplicity
2. Readability
3. Fast implementation
4. Type safety
5. Maintainability
6. Good user experience

Do not introduce enterprise-level architecture unless there is a concrete requirement.

Avoid premature abstractions.

## Architecture

Prefer a simple structure:

frontend/
└── src/
   ├── components/
   ├── pages/
   ├── hooks/
   ├── services/
   ├── types/
   ├── theme/
   ├── App.tsx
   └── main.tsx

Create additional directories only when they provide clear value.

## Components

Use small, focused functional components.

A component should have a clear responsibility.

Avoid:

- Giant components
- Deeply nested JSX
- Duplicated UI logic
- Unnecessary wrapper components
- Generic abstractions without reuse

## State

Prefer local React state.

Do not introduce Redux, Zustand or another state management library unless the MVP has a concrete requirement for it.

Keep state as close as possible to where it is used.

## Business Logic

Keep complex logic outside presentation components.

Prefer:

Component
    ↓
Custom Hook
    ↓
Service
    ↓
FastAPI

Simple UI state can remain inside the component.

## Code Quality

Prefer explicit and readable code.

Avoid:

- `any`
- Dead code
- Unused imports
- Magic values
- Unnecessary comments
- Unnecessary abstractions
- Unnecessary dependencies

Do not refactor unrelated code while implementing a feature.

## Accessibility

Interactive elements must be keyboard accessible.

Forms must have accessible labels.

Icon-only buttons must have accessible labels.

Do not use color as the only way to communicate state.

## Responsive Design

The application must work on:

- Mobile
- Tablet
- Desktop

Prefer responsive Material UI layout primitives and breakpoints.

## Error Handling

User-facing operations must handle:

- Loading
- Success
- Empty
- Error

Do not expose internal exceptions or stack traces to users.